From 22e435d72f0920898a5a2fe68b3d6c15eec2cb7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 14:39:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Net play: the pc that CLICKS is the host, the pc it clicked
 is player 2

Both machines came up as player 2, each waiting for the other to press
START. The old rule made the dialler the client and the dialled pc the
host, which is the opposite of what the panel invites: the person who
clicked is the one sitting there expecting something to happen next, and
on the other pc nothing had visibly changed, so they clicked too.

Which end opened the TCP connection is not a game role - the lockstep is
symmetric and net_peer_socket() is the same one socket on both sides - so
the role can go to whoever it is most useful to. Now: CLICK the other pc
and YOU are the host, with the mission arrows and START; the pc you
clicked becomes player 2 by itself and its panel says so, and that it has
nothing to click.

If both click each other in the same instant, the session-id tie-break
runs as before but the other way up (lower id keeps its dial and hosts;
the other drops its dial and keeps the inbound one, so both are on the
same wire). And if that has nothing to go on - a typed-in address that
never shouted - the first START to arrive settles it: a host receiving a
start becomes player 2 on the socket it arrived on, and a host already in
a game ignores it and logs it.

